<UserControl x:Class="Plith.DropCatcher.Shelf.ShelfSurface"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             mc:Ignorable="d"
             Focusable="False"
             UseLayoutRounding="True"
             SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
             TextOptions.TextFormattingMode="Ideal"
             TextOptions.TextRenderingMode="ClearType">

    <!-- The shelf page, as a UserControl rather than a Window: a render harness can instantiate
         and photograph this, and it cannot do that with a layered Window. See CatcherWindow for
         the shape this stands beside: that one is the brief prompt shown mid-drag, this is the
         page a click into the resting shelf opens.

         No dictionary from Plith is merged here, deliberately. This project does not reference
         Plith.csproj (see Plith.DropCatcher.csproj's own comment on why: it must stay small and
         must not share Plith's startup), so pack://application:,,,/Plith;component/... would
         resolve only inside the render harness, which loads Plith.dll itself, and would fail
         silently the moment this runs as the real catcher process. The handful of icon shapes
         below are duplicated literal geometry rather than a shared resource for that reason, the
         same reason NotchGeometry.cs is LINKED rather than referenced: a real dependency on
         Plith's assembly is not available to pay for it.

         Every colour brush used below is resolved from THIS control's own Resources, filled in
         by Apply(ShelfPalette) at runtime, under the same keys Plith uses for the same things
         (OsdSurfaceBrush, NotchInk, NotchInkMuted, NotchTrack, AccentBrush). Nothing here reaches
         through Application.Resources: this process has no application-level theme, and the
         render harness proved once already (ShelfWidget) that anything reaching through
         Application renders in colours the product never shows. -->

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <!-- The header buttons' own template.

             They had NONE, which is how "hover makes things disappear" happens: a Button with
             Background="Transparent" and no template still uses WPF's default chrome, and that
             chrome paints a light system fill on hover with a system-coloured border. On this
             panel it swallowed a white glyph and grey text. Reported from a real session.

             Declared here rather than taken from Plith's dictionary for the reason this file's
             header comment gives: this project does not reference Plith.csproj, so a pack:// URI
             would resolve in the render harness and fail in the real catcher process. The hover
             and pressed washes are the same values the notch's own controls use.

             Apply(ShelfPalette) merges the palette into this same Resources collection at
             runtime, and a merge does not disturb what is declared here. -->
        <Style x:Key="HeaderButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <!-- Transparent, not null: WPF hit-tests a Transparent brush and does not
                             hit-test a null one, so a null background here would leave the button
                             answering only where its glyph paints. -->
                        <Border x:Name="Chrome"
                                CornerRadius="7"
                                Background="Transparent"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Chrome" Property="Background" Value="#1FFFFFFF" />
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Chrome" Property="Background" Value="#1FFFFFFF" />
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Chrome" Property="Background" Value="#33FFFFFF" />
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- The per-tile remove chip, which is NOT the header buttons with a smaller radius.

             A header button sits on the card's own surface, so a wash is enough to show it has
             been entered. This one sits ON a tile, on top of a file icon that may be any colour
             a shell icon can be, and it appeared with no ground at all: a 1.6-thick light stroke
             over a bright icon, which is the state reported from a real session as the hover
             "losing things". A wash over an unknown ground cannot be relied on to show anything.

             So the chip carries its own opaque ground from the moment it appears, and hover and
             press lighten THAT rather than the tile under it. The ring around it separates the
             circle from a dark icon, the case a dark ground alone would disappear into.

             The colours are literal rather than palette keys because the notch palette has no
             scrim family, and they are dark in both themes on purpose: NotchInk is #F2F5F8 in
             Light as well as Dark, so this surface is dark whichever theme is chosen, and a chip
             that flipped with the theme would carry light ink on a light ground. -->
        <Style x:Key="TileRemoveButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
            <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Chip"
                                CornerRadius="9"
                                Background="#D91B2027"
                                BorderBrush="#40FFFFFF"
                                BorderThickness="1"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Chip" Property="Background" Value="#F23A444F" />
                                <Setter TargetName="Chip" Property="BorderBrush" Value="#73FFFFFF" />
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Chip" Property="Background" Value="#F23A444F" />
                                <Setter TargetName="Chip" Property="BorderBrush" Value="#73FFFFFF" />
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Chip" Property="Background" Value="#FF4C5864" />
                                <Setter TargetName="Chip" Property="BorderBrush" Value="#A6FFFFFF" />
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- Only the BOTTOM corners are round, the same rule NotchGeometry.BottomRoundedClip states
         for the notch itself: this page grows out of the notch and is anchored at the same top
         screen edge (OpenShelf carries the same X/Y/W/H rectangle Show does), and rounding the
         top would carve two notches of desktop out of the edge the shape is part of. -->
    <Border x:Name="Surface"
            CornerRadius="0,0,16,16"
            Background="{DynamicResource OsdSurfaceBrush}"
            BorderThickness="1"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource NotchTrack}">
        <Grid Margin="16">
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                <RowDefinition Height="*" />
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <!-- Header: a title, and the one shelf-wide action left. Clear is a real Button
                 rather than a pressable Border, so Tab and a screen reader reach it the same way
                 any other control in the product is reached. The tiles below cannot be, because a
                 tile's action (select, then drag) has no equivalent gesture on a keyboard, which
                 is the same reason ShelfWidget's tiles are Borders and not Buttons. -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="0" Margin="0,0,0,12">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                           Text="Shelf"
                           FontSize="13"
                           FontWeight="SemiBold"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource NotchInk}" />

                <!-- Two controls where there was one, and the one was wrong.

                     The ✕ used to CLEAR the shelf. Reported from a real session as "pressing the
                     X does not close it", which is the kinder half of the problem: a ✕ in the
                     top-right corner of a window means close to everyone who has used Windows,
                     so the press that was meant to put the shelf away emptied it instead.

                     Clear says the word. It is the destructive one, it is rare, and a word cannot
                     be mistaken for the close box. -->
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal">
                    <Button x:Name="ClearButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource HeaderButtonStyle}"
                            Height="24"
                            Padding="8,0"
                            Margin="0,0,4,0"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Clear the shelf"
                            Click="OnClearClick">
                        <TextBlock Text="Clear"
                                   FontSize="11"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource NotchInkMuted}" />
                    </Button>

                    <Button x:Name="CloseButton"
                            Style="{StaticResource HeaderButtonStyle}"
                            Width="28" Height="28"
                            Padding="0"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Close the shelf"
                            Click="OnCloseClick">
                        <!-- Same coordinates as PlithIcons' IconClose, duplicated: see the header
                             comment. -->
                        <Path Data="M6,6 L18,18 M18,6 L6,18"
                              Width="14" Height="14"
                              Stretch="Uniform"
                              Stroke="{DynamicResource NotchInk}"
                              StrokeThickness="1.6"
                              StrokeStartLineCap="Round"
                              StrokeEndLineCap="Round" />
                    </Button>
                </StackPanel>
            </Grid>

            <!-- The stacks, built in code the same way ShelfWidget builds its tiles: each one
                 needs its own AutomationProperties.Name and its own press handler, which an
                 ItemsControl template cannot give an individual generated element.

                 AllowDrop lives here, on the container, rather than on each built column: a
                 drop past the last stack has to land somewhere too (it means "start a new
                 stack"), and there is no column there to carry the flag. OnColumnsDrop works
                 out which column, if any, the drop point actually fell inside. -->
            <!-- The host is what catches the drop; Columns is what the eye sees.

                 They are two elements because they want opposite widths. Columns is centred, so
                 a shelf holding two stacks sits in the middle of the panel instead of hugging
                 the left edge with the rest of the card empty beside it. But the drop target has
                 to stay the FULL width: releasing a tile in the empty area past the last stack is
                 how a new stack is made, and a panel shrunk to its content would stop receiving
                 exactly the release that gesture needs.

                 So the handlers live out here and report positions in the host's coordinates.
                 TargetStackIndex translates each column into the same space, which is the only
                 reason it can still decide which column, if any, a point fell inside. -->
            <Grid x:Name="ColumnsHost"
                  Grid.Row="1"
                  VerticalAlignment="Top"
                  Background="Transparent"
                  AllowDrop="False">
                <StackPanel x:Name="Columns"
                            Orientation="Horizontal"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                            VerticalAlignment="Top" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- The empty state's own host, a SIBLING of ColumnsHost rather than a child of it,
                 so its dashed box can stretch to the whole row. ColumnsHost is top-aligned and
                 therefore only as tall as the tiles in it; a box stretched inside that would
                 stretch to nothing.

                 The box used to be a fixed 136 DIP, which is the two-row height an empty shelf
                 OPENS at. That is right when it opens empty and wrong the moment a full shelf is
                 cleared while it is up: the frame is chosen once at open by design (see
                 NotchGeometry.ShelfFrameFor), so clearing a three-row shelf left a 283 DIP window
                 with a 136 DIP box hanging in the top of it. Reported from a real session. -->
            <Grid x:Name="EmptyHost" Grid.Row="1" Visibility="Collapsed" />
        </Grid>
    </Border>
</UserControl>
